I lead the flight module team at Metis and mentor junior developers. A team with juniors and interns can ship real work and grow fast at the same time, if the setup gives both sides something.

The juniors get real experience and visible progress. The team gets more done, and over time it gets engineers who know the product deeply. Here's the setup I use.

## Real, scoped tasks

Juniors learn the most from production work, cut small enough to finish. Busywork teaches them that their work doesn't matter. A task that's too big teaches them to get stuck.

I scope each task so a junior can finish it within days, with a clear definition of done. It ships to users like everyone else's work.

## Ownership early

Once someone has shipped a few tasks, I give them a small area to own end to end: a screen, an integration detail, a part of the test suite. Owning something changes how people work. They start asking why it's built that way, and they notice when it breaks.

## Pairing on the hard parts

Some problems are too hard to learn from a ticket. For those, we pair. I drive at first and explain what I'm looking at and why; then they drive and I ask questions. Pairing passes on the parts that aren't written down anywhere: how to read an unfamiliar codebase, how to debug a supplier error, when to stop and ask.

## Code review as teaching

A review that only says "change this" fixes the code and teaches nothing. In my reviews I explain the reason behind each comment: the bug it prevents, the convention it follows, the trade-off behind it. Juniors start applying the reason to code I'll never review.

## What the team gets back

This setup costs senior time up front, in scoping, pairing and longer reviews. It pays back in a team that ships more each month, needs less supervision, and keeps its knowledge when people move on. The product is protected because every change still goes through review.
